{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Let us make man.</b> The humility of the Holy One, blessed be He, we learn from here — since man is in the likeness of the angels and they would be envious of him, therefore He took counsel with them. And when He judges kings, He takes counsel with His retinue — for so we find in Ahab, for Micah said to him: ״רָאִיתִי אֶת ה' יֹשֵׁב עַל כִּסְאוֹ וְכָל צְבָא הַשָּׁמַיִם עוֹמְדִים עָלָיו מִימִינוֹ וּמִשְּׂמֹאלוֹ״ (1 Kings 22) — and is there right and left before Him? Rather, these advocate for merit and these for liability. And so in ״בִּגְזֵרַת עִירִין פִּתְגָמָא וּבְמֵאַמַר קַדִּישִׁין שְׁאֵלְתָא״ (Daniel 4) — here too, with His retinue, He sought permission. He said to them: There are among the upper beings those in My likeness; if there are none in My likeness among the lower beings, see — there is jealousy in the work of Creation." "<b>Let us make man.</b> Even though they did not assist Him in his creation, and there is room for the heretics to exploit it, Scripture did not refrain from teaching derech eretz and the trait of humility — that the great one should take counsel and seek permission from the lesser one. And if He had written ״אֶעֱשֶׂה אָדָם,״ we would not have learned that He was speaking with His court, but with Himself. And the refutation of the heretics is written at its side: ״וַיִּבְרָא אֶת הָאָדָם״ — and it does not write וַיִּבְרְאוּ." "<b>In our image.</b> In our mold." "<b>As our likeness.</b> To understand and to discern." "<b>And rule over the fish of the sea.</b> This expression has the sense of dominion and the sense of descent. If he merits — he rules over the beasts and the animals. If he does not merit — he becomes lowly before them, and the beast rules over him."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:57:14.883233779Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}